{"id": "124", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4\" and set and level them according to the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". Assemble the floor and place it on the skids as indicated in the floor framing section of the plans. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. Cut two more 4 x 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids and, when the frame is square, toenail the floor joists to the skids. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. The size of the shed can be adjusted as needed.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves: 1. Preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel and cutting two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, then setting and leveling them. 2. Cutting two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4″ and six joists to 44 3/4″, assembling the floor, setting it on the skids, checking for square, and anchoring the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. 3. Alternatively, cutting two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to 6 feet for skids, positioning the frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists to the skids when square, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and constructing walls separately before raising them onto the frame. 4. The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with the ability to expand in increments of 2 or 4 feet, and floor framing requires cutting two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to 6 feet for rim joists.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves: 1. Preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel and cutting two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, then setting and leveling them. 2. Cutting two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4″ and six joists to 44 3/4″, assembling the floor, setting it on the skids, checking for square, and anchoring the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. 3. Alternatively, cutting two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to 6 feet for skids, positioning the frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists to the skids when square, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and constructing walls separately before raising them onto the frame. 4. The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with the ability to expand in increments of 2 or 4 feet, and floor framing requires cutting two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to 6 feet for rim joists.", "time_sec": 20.563, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 35957, 1408, 1278, 4265, 42679, 1044, 7654, 1261, 16987, 6793, 42878, 19263, 1058, 1032, 1049, 1046, 90327, 1302, 1278, 5586, 1454, 1261, 1032, 1052, 12000, 6240, 1307, 23070, 1286, 59396, 1321, 23589, 2295, 1032, 1052, 2460, 1032, 1052, 3044, 4807, 1317, 1032, 1055, 1048, 1032, 1050, 1047, 1052, 12000, 1044, 2430, 9436, 1321, 3178, 1302, 2424, 1046, 1032, 1050, 1046, 42698, 3333, 2295, 1032, 1050, 2460, 1032, 1054, 17211, 5552, 3742, 1317, 1032, 1055, 1032, 1051, 1047, 1052, 12000, 1321, 5946, 5552, 3742, 1317, 1032, 1052, 1052, 1032, 1051, 1047, 1052, 12000, 1044, 112443, 1278, 11237, 1044, 9436, 1494, 1408, 1278, 3044, 4807, 1044, 24124, 1394, 12333, 1044, 1321, 126838, 1278, 6448, 1317, 1278, 3044, 4807, 1454, 3946, 5552, 1416, 29446, 28472, 1046, 1032, 1051, 1046, 37346, 1044, 23589, 2295, 6909, 30702, 1032, 1052, 1536, 1032, 1052, 1115, 1317, 1032, 1054, 10509, 1394, 3044, 4807, 1044, 36989, 1278, 6448, 1408, 1278, 3044, 4807, 1044, 46819, 42380, 11237, 5552, 3742, 1317, 1278, 3044, 4807, 2200, 12333, 1044, 85639, 1032, 1051, 1