{"id": "127", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Sure! Here's my attempt to answer your question based on the provided passages:\n\nHow do you prepare the site for building a lean-to shed?\n\nAccording to passage 1, step 1, you should prepare the site by laying down a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. This will provide a stable base for the shed's foundation. Additionally, passage 2 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids, which will serve as the foundation for the shed. Finally, passage 3 provides instructions for building the shed's floor, including cutting rim joists to a length of 6 feet. Based on these passages, it appears that preparing the site involves laying down a layer of gravel and setting the skids in place before constructing the floor.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves: 1. Preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel and cutting two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, then setting and leveling them. 2. Cutting two 2 x 6 rim joists to 7 3/4″ and six joists to 44 3/4″, assembling the floor, setting it on the skids, checking for square, and anchoring the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. 3. Alternatively, cutting two pressure-treated 4 by 4s to 6 feet for skids, positioning the frame on the skids, toenailing floor joists to the skids when square, attaching 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, and constructing walls separately before raising them onto the frame. 4. The shed dimensions are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, with the ability to expand in increments of 2 or 4 feet, and floor framing requires cutting two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to 6 feet for rim joists.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, building a lean-to shed involves: 1. Preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel and cutting two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4″, then sett