{"id": "206", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, Swiss chard (also known as just plain chard) is a type of beet plant that has been selected for its leaf production rather than storage root formation. It produces large, tender deep green leaves with thick, crisp stalks. The leaves have a more assertive and sometimes bitter taste compared to spinach. Swiss chard is a perishable vegetable and should be stored in the refrigerator. It can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, as stated in passage 3. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, as described in passage 1. Chard is cultivated as a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, as noted in passage 2. It can be harvested when leaves are young and tender or after maturity when stems are tougher, and is extremely perishable, requiring storage in unwashed form in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days, as detailed in passage 3.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation, as stated in passage 3. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, as described in passage 1. Chard is cultivated as a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) and is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, as noted in passage 2. It can be harvested when leaves are young and tender or after maturity when stems are tougher, and is extremely perishable, requiring storage in unwashed form in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days, as detailed in passage 3.", "time_sec": 12.959, "nemotron": {"r