{"id": "341", "question": "how to make ribs", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can make ribs:\n\nStep 1: Prepare the ribs\n\n* Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs (passage 3)\n* Season the ribs with a dry rub and mustard mixture (passage 2)\n\nStep 2: Grill the ribs\n\n* Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium heat (passage 1)\n* Place the ribs meaty-side up on the grill and brush with some of the sauce (passage 1)\n* Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs with more sauce (passage 1)\n* Continue to cook and flip every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through (passage 1)\n\nTotal grilling time: 20 minutes\n\nNote: The passages do not mention the temperature at which to cook the ribs, but a medium heat of 350°F to 450°F is suggested.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make ribs: 1Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). 2Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another 5 minutes. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes. 3Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer. This arrangement allows for heat circulation on all sides of the ribs. 4Season the ribs: Mix the mustard and the liquid smoke, if using, and brush the ribs on both sides. Sprinkle the ribs with the dry rub and pat gently to make sure the rub adheres to the rib meat. 5Oven-cooked BBQ Ribs: 1 Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs using a knife to start it near the narrow end and pull off in one piece. 2 Put the ribs in a resealable bag and add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover. 3 Line the baking pan with heavy duty foil, using plenty of overlapping foil to make a tent.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_catego