{"id": "451", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically the result of meteorite impacts.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically the result of meteorite impacts.", "time_sec": 6.994, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 35957, 1408, 1278, 4265, 42679, 1044, 1261, 2615, 101948, 1395, 1261, 4329, 1319, 1269, 4403, 1925, 4661, 5669, 1574, 23455, 18596, 7563, 1513, 1278, 3977, 1307, 1261, 88781, 2200, 1278, 122993, 16413, 5418, 60764, 1044, 3016, 1261, 110088, 1395, 1261, 39922, 2479, 5194, 1513, 1278, 3977, 1307, 1261, 88781, 3879, 1278, 10431, 1455, 1395, 2434, 1394, 104159, 1307, 122993, 1321, 81021, 104