{"id": "452", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression made due to volcanic activity, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano that allows for the eruption of magma and lava. The primary difference between the two is the method of formation. Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large depression, while craters are formed by impacts from meteorites.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically the result of meteorite impacts.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for eruption of magma and lava, formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure, and is typically the result of meteorite impacts.", "time_sec": 7.419, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 35957, 1408, 1278, 4265, 42679, 1044, 1261, 2615, 101948, 1395, 1261, 4329, 1319, 1269, 4403, 1925, 4661, 5669, 1574, 23455, 18596, 7563, 1513, 1278, 3977, 1307, 1261, 88781, 2200, 1278, 122993, 16413, 5418, 60764, 1044, 3016, 1261, 110088, 1395, 1261, 39922, 2479, 5194, 1513, 1278, 3977, 1307, 1261, 88781, 3879, 1278, 10431, 1455, 1395, 2434, 1394, 104159, 130