{"id": "492", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using Favotter.net or Favstar.fm, which are websites that track favorited tweets and show you who favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you, or by going to Twitter.com, logging in, clicking your name in the upper right corner, selecting Settings, then clicking the blue Notifications link to see notifications of who favorited your tweets.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, you can tell who liked your tweet by using Favotter.net or Favstar.fm, which are websites that track favorited tweets and show you who favorited your tweets or the same tweets as you, or by going to Twitter.com, logging in, clicking your name in the upper right corner, selecting Settings, then clicking the blue Notifications link to see notifications of who favorited your tweets.", "time_sec": 14.087, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [10