{"id": "510", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that the main difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is their location within the sirloin muscle. Sirloin steaks are cut from the back of the animal and continue off the short loin from which other cuts such as T-bone and porterhouse steaks are made. On the other hand, porterhouse steaks are specifically carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin and therefore have more of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-bone steaks. However, the passages suggest that both sirloin and porterhouse steaks may be referred to as the same thing or as a large version of the T-bone due to their similarity in appearance and taste.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet, making it a combination of the fillet and front of the sirloin (short loin) on either side of a T-shaped bone.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is cut from further back on the sirloin and contains a larger portion of the tenderloin or fillet, making it a combination of the fillet and front of the sirloin (short loin) on either side of a T-shaped bone.", "time_sec": 11.768, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_proce