{"id": "668", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is what happens when you breathe in and out:\n\nWhen you breathe in (inhalation):\n\n* The intercostal muscles contract, causing the ribcage to expand.\n* The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\n* Pressure inside the chest is lowered, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation):\n\n* The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n* The intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity.\n* Air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nSo, in summary, when you breathe in, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract to bring air into the lungs, and when you breathe out, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax to force air out of the lungs.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity while the intercostal muscles between the ribs relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth, which requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity; when you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, the diaphragm contracts pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, lowering the pressure inside the chest and sucking air into the lungs, and outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) into your lungs, with a hole in the chest wall allowing air to enter through that hole, causing air to gather around the lung and collapse it when there is a large amount of air around the outside of the lung preventing expansion.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity while the intercostal muscles between the ribs relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth, which requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity; when you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, the diaphragm contracts pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, lowering the pressure inside the chest and sucking air into the lungs, and outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) into your lungs, with a hole in the chest wall allowing air to enter through that hole, causing air to gather around the lung and collapse it when there is a large amount of air around the outside of the lung preventing expansion.", "time_sec": 24.294, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 35957, 1408, 1278, 4265, 42679, 1044, 2200, 1636, 56336, 1848, 1044, 2143, 82410, 18110, 1264, 1321, 20183, 40022, 2203, 1278, 15448, 27927, 3016, 1278, 35325, 80715, 24812, 2396, 1278, 63484, 18110, 1317, 9980, 1278, 5702, 1294, 1278, 15448, 27927, 1044, 37630, 4864, 12478, 1294, 10006, 42812, 1848, 1307, 2143, 40707, 1044, 12330, 42712, 1044, 1321, 2430, 1848, 1307, 2143, 24917, 1505, 11504, 1044, 1799, 10867, 1836, 8267, 15388, 1636, 1736, 1261, 12025, 6137, 1505, 1584, 6965, 8137, 5177, 1059, 2200, 1636, 56336, 1294, 1044, 1278, 35325, 80715, 24812, 8633, 1317, 10959, 1278, 16120, 1099, 1541, 1044, 1278, 82410, 36010, 29886, 3151, 4969, 1317, 5597, 1278, 8167, 1307, 1278, 15448, 1044, 53943, 1278, 6909, 6625, 1278, 15448, 1321, 107492, 4864, 2203, 1278, 40707, 1044, 1321, 7805, 4864, 77697, 2203, 2143, 12330, 42712, 1319, 1116, 30630, 1097, 1041, 2203, 2143, 40707, 1044, 1454, 1261, 18441, 