{"id": "669", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the given passages:\n\nWhen you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to enter the lungs. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract, expanding the ribcage and further increasing the space in the chest cavity. Air enters the lungs through the windpipe (trachea) and fills the expanded lung tissue.\n\nWhen you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, allowing the chest cavity to decrease in size. As the space in the chest cavity decreases, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nTherefore, when you breathe in and out, air enters the lungs during inhalation and leaves the lungs during exhalation.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity while the intercostal muscles between the ribs relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth, which requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity; when you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, the diaphragm contracts pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, lowering the pressure inside the chest and sucking air into the lungs, and outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) into your lungs, with a hole in the chest wall allowing air to enter through that hole, causing air to gather around the lung and collapse it when there is a large amount of air around the outside of the lung preventing expansion.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_nemotron", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity while the intercostal muscles between the ribs relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs, windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth, which requires no effort unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity; when you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, the diaphragm contracts pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, lowering the pressure inside the chest and sucking air into the lungs, and outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) into your lungs, with a hole in the chest wall allowing air to enter through that hole, causing air to gather around the lung and collapse it when there is a large amount of air around the outside of the lung preventing expansion.", "time_sec": 23.546, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 35957, 1408, 1278, 4265, 42679, 1044, 2200, 1636, 56336, 1848, 1044, 2143, 82410, 18110, 1264, 1321, 20183, 40022, 2203, 1278, 15448, 27927, 3016, 1278, 35325, 80715, 24812, 2396, 1278, 63484, 18110, 1317, 9980, 1278, 5702, 1294, 1278, 15448, 27927, 1044, 37630, 4864, 12478, 1294, 10006, 42812, 1848, 1307, 2143, 40707, 1044, 12330, 42712, 1044, 1321, 2430, 1848, 1307, 2143, 24917, 1505, 11504, 1044, 1799, 10867, 1836, 8267, 15388, 1636, 1736, 1261, 12025, 6137, 1505, 1584, 6965, 8137, 5177, 1059, 2200, 1636, 56336, 1294, 1044, 1278, 35325, 80715, 24812, 8633, 1317, 10959, 1278, 16120, 1099, 1541, 1044, 1278, 82410, 36010, 29886, 3151, 4969, 1317, 5597, 1278, 8167, 1307, 1278, 15448, 1044, 53943, 1278, 6909, 6625, 1278, 15448, 1321, 107492, 4864, 2203, 1278, 40707, 1044, 1321, 7805, 4864, 77697,